{
  "name": "Content/Component Models",
  "slug": "content-component-models",
  "domain": "components",
  "source": {
    "kind": "code-only",
    "why": "No Figma set. Its contract depends on required props and CSS state, neither of which a Figma variant can express.",
    "component": "src/components/ComponentModels.tsx",
    "styles": "src/components/ComponentModels.css"
  },
  "description": "The rebuild that was abandoned partway through, drawn beside the thing that\nreplaced it: four design languages inside every component, against one base\ncomponent with brand tokens on top.\n\nIT ILLUSTRATES A REJECTED EXPLORATION, which decides its shape, the same way\nthe governance ladder's shape follows from illustrating one. The reader has\njust been told that every component carried four sets of decisions and that\nevery later change had to be correct in four places at once. That sentence is\na count, and a count is a thing to show rather than a thing to say, so the\ncontrol marks the row a change lands on and the reader finds it four times on\nthe left and once on the right.\n\nTHE FIDELITY COST IS DRAWN TOO, and it has to be. The exploration admits in\nwords that the pivot cost some fidelity, and a picture where the right-hand\nmodel is all upside would be arguing with the paragraph beside it. So exactly\none decision moves out to the brand chips: what shipped lets a brand differ on\nhow a component looks and not on how it is built, and that is visible before\nanybody touches the control.\n\nNO COLOUR OF ITS OWN, like the governance figure and unlike the token-tiers\none. This depicts a structure rather than somebody else's palette, so every\ntone is a token, it repaints with the brand pack, and both themes come out\nright without a second export or a second set of values.\n\nInteractive with no JavaScript, on the same mechanism: one native checkbox and\n`:has()`. `:has()` is what makes it possible at all, since the thing being\nmarked sits below the control in the document but the marks are spread across\ntwo columns that `~` could never reach into.",
  "donts": [
    "Do not mix a wash with a band token. `color-mix(<token> n%, var(--semantic-band-sunken))` paints one specific band's fill, and this figure ships on a base band and renders against all four in Storybook. Mix with `transparent` so the wash composites over whatever it lands on.",
    "Do not mark a change to the look. It lands in one place per brand in both models, so marking it shows two structures agreeing and proves nothing. The comparison only works on a decision the shipped model holds once.",
    "Do not draw the shipped model as all upside. The exploration admits in words that the pivot cost fidelity, so exactly one decision has to move out to the brand chips; a picture without it argues with the paragraph beside it.",
    "Do not let the marked row change size. It takes a border when marked, so an unmarked row reserves a transparent one; without it every row in the figure shifts a pixel and the diagram reads as twitching.",
    "Do not label every mark. Five copies of the same two words read as part of the structure rather than as an annotation on it, and the control above already names what is being changed.",
    "Never let the toggle be anything but a real checkbox. The space bar, the focus ring and the announced state come free from the browser, and a div with a click handler has none of them.",
    "Never put `role=\"img\"` over the control. It labels the two MODELS, which are a picture; a checkbox inside a region announced as an image is a control assistive technology has been told to skip."
  ],
  "props": {
    "alt": {
      "type": "string",
      "required": true,
      "note": "The accessible name for the whole figure, and REQUIRED for the same reason `IconButton` requires `aria-label` and the other two diagrams require `alt`. It carries more weight here than on either of them, because the argument this picture makes is a COUNT: four marks on the left against one on the right. A sighted reader counts them; the alt has to say the number."
    },
    "subject": {
      "type": "string",
      "required": true,
      "note": "The component the picture is drawn about. \"Date picker\"."
    },
    "languages": {
      "type": "string[]",
      "required": true,
      "note": "The four design languages, left to right and top to bottom."
    },
    "decisions": {
      "type": "ComponentDecision[]",
      "required": true,
      "note": "What a component carries. One of them is `perBrand`, which is what moves to the right-hand model's token chips, and one is `changes`, which is what the control marks."
    },
    "models": {
      "type": "{ abandoned: ComponentModelLabels; shipped: ComponentModelLabels }",
      "required": true,
      "note": "The two headers. Abandoned first: it was the plan first."
    },
    "base": {
      "type": "string",
      "required": true,
      "note": "The eyebrow on the shipped component, naming whose component it is."
    },
    "change": {
      "type": "ComponentModelsChange",
      "required": false,
      "note": "The change, as a control rather than a caption. Optional: without it the figure is the two structures and nothing else, which is the right thing to render anywhere the cost of a change is not the subject."
    },
    "defaultChanged": {
      "type": "boolean",
      "required": false,
      "note": "Start with the change showing. Off by default, because the structures are the honest resting state and the count is what a reader goes looking for. It exists so a story can snapshot the second state, since Chromatic photographs a page rather than operating one — the same reason `GovernanceTiers` takes a `defaultRejected`."
    },
    "caption": {
      "type": "string",
      "required": false
    },
    "captionCopyRef": {
      "type": "string",
      "required": false,
      "note": "`<file>:<path>` into src/copy, making the caption editable in the browser under `npm run dev`. Dev tooling only; inert in a build."
    }
  },
